  "note": "Roman to Limbu (Sirijanga). Limbu has no virama: a syllable-closing consonant takes its dedicated small letter, and independent vowels are written on the vowel-carrier letter. Two phonemic contrasts carry dedicated keys because the script encodes them and losing them merges distinct words: \"q\" writes mukphreng for the glottal stop, which contrasts with final /p t k/ (van Driem tradition; Chhatthare Limbu grammar gives [hap] 'he weeps' vs [ha?] 'he bites'), and \":\" after a vowel writes kemphreng for length. Chhatthare Limbu has no length contrast but the other dialects do, so the key is offered for the dialects that need it rather than withheld because one dialect does not. Subjoined ya/ra/wa clusters and sa-i are not covered in v0.",
